|~ What is declared under `::number`: the refusal of its
   constructor, and the names that run on a number. A name holds its
   page and its primitive, the parts of the object of its call are
   declared under it, each with the law a part keeps, and the refusals
   its primitive raises sit under it too. ~|

:number/construct |~~ Reads a number as itself, a finite double. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/PayloadNotNumberError
  |~~ `::number` reads its payload as the number it is; a payload of
      another kind is refused, and text is read by `parseJson`.
      ~(
        ::number"5"
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::number/PayloadNotNumberError ::string]
       )
  ~~|

:number/NotFiniteError
  |~~ A number is a finite double: an answer brought under `::number`
      that left their range, a sum or a product too large, is refused
      here, whichever word computed it, and the trail names the step and
      the subject it ran on.

      ~(1e308 | mul 10 | add 1 !| [type (/trail * /step)] | eq [::number/NotFiniteError [~(mul 10)]])
  ~~|

:number/add
  |~~ Adds a number to the subject: `a | add b` computes a + b. A vector
      adds element by element, `* add 1`, and sums by `sum`; a string is
      joined by `append`; two numbers one value holds add by `sumOf`,
      `{:x 10 :y 3} | sumOf /x /y`. ::call~(add 3)

      ~(10 | add 3 | eq 13)
      A subject that is no number is refused under the name:
      ~("hello" | add 1 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError ::string])
      An object with more numbers than the call has places is refused:
      ~(1 | add 1 2 !| type | eq ::number/add/ObjectBeyondPartsError)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/add/addend
  |~~ The number added to the subject.

      ~(1 | add "x" !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/add/addend/NotNumberError ::string])
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:number/add/addend/NotNumberError |~~ The addend is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/add/sum |~~ The subject and the addend added. ~~|
  ::number

:number/sub
  |~~ Subtracts a number from the subject, the minuend. A vector
      subtracts element by element, `* sub 1`; two numbers one value
      holds subtract by `subOf`, `{:total 10 :paid 3} | subOf /total
      /paid`. ::call~(sub 3)

      ~(10 | sub 3 | eq 7)
      ~("hello" | sub 1 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/sub/SubjectNotNumberError ::string])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/sub/subtrahend
  |~~ The number subtracted from the subject.

      ~(1 | sub "x" !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/sub/subtrahend/NotNumberError ::string])
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:number/sub/subtrahend/NotNumberError |~~ The subtrahend is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/sub/difference |~~ The subject less the subtrahend. ~~|
  ::number

:number/mul
  |~~ Multiplies the subject by a number. A vector multiplies element by
      element, `* mul 2`; two numbers one value holds multiply by
      `mulOf`, `{:price 4 :qty 3} | mulOf /price /qty`. ::call~(mul 3)

      ~(10 | mul 3 | eq 30)
      ~("hello" | mul 2 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/mul/SubjectNotNumberError ::string])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/mul/multiplier
  |~~ The number the subject is multiplied by.

      ~(1 | mul "x" !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/mul/multiplier/NotNumberError ::string])
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:number/mul/multiplier/NotNumberError |~~ The multiplier is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/mul/product |~~ The subject times the multiplier. ~~|
  ::number

:number/div
  |~~ Divides the subject, the dividend, by a number. A vector divides
      element by element, `* div 2`; two numbers one value holds divide
      by `divOf`, `{:sum 20 :count 4} | divOf /sum /count`.
      ::call~(div 2)

      ~(10 | div 2 | eq 5)
      ~("hello" | div 2 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/div/SubjectNotNumberError ::string])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/div/divisor
  |~~ The number the subject is divided by.

      ~(1 | div "x" !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/div/divisor/NotNumberError ::string])
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:number/div/divisor/NotNumberError |~~ The divisor is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/div/ByZeroError
  |~~ A division by zero has no finite answer; a divisor that may be
      zero is tested first, `if (/count | eq 0) ~(0) ~(divOf /sum /count)`.

      ~(10 | div 0 !| type | eq ::number/div/ByZeroError)
  ~~|

:number/div/quotient |~~ The subject divided by the divisor. ~~|
  ::number

:number/round
  |~~ The number rounded half away from zero to the digits after the
      point the call gives, none when it gives none; a negative count
      rounds to tens and beyond. A string holding a number is read first
      by `parseJson`. ::call~(round 2)

      ~(3.14159 | round 2 | eq 3.14)
      ~(2.5 | round | eq 3)
      ~(-2.5 | round | eq -3)
      ~(1234 | round -2 | eq 1200)
      ~(-0.4 | round | print | eq "0")
      ~("2.5" | round !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/round/SubjectNotNumberError ::string])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/round/digits
  |~~ The digits after the point, a whole number, none when the call
      gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0 0)

:number/round/digits/NotNumberError |~~ The digits is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/round/DigitsNotIntegerError
  |~~ The digits of `round` are a whole number, `round 2`.

      ~(2.5 | round 0.5 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::number/round/DigitsNotIntegerError ::number])
  ~~|

:number/round/rounded |~~ The number rounded to the digits. ~~|
  ::number

:number/gt
  |~~ Whether the subject is greater than the number the call gives.

      ~(10 | gt 5 | eq true)
      ~(null | gt 1 !| /addresses | has ::number/gt)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/gt/than
  |~~ The value the subject is compared with. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:number/gt/than/NotNumberError |~~ The than is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/gt/greater |~~ Whether the subject comes after the value compared. ~~|
  ::boolean

:number/gte
  |~~ Whether the subject is greater than or equal to the number the
      call gives.

      ~(10 | gte 10 | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/gte/than
  |~~ The value the subject is compared with. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:number/gte/than/NotNumberError |~~ The than is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/gte/atLeast |~~ Whether the subject comes after the value compared or equals it. ~~|
  ::boolean

:number/lt
  |~~ Whether the subject is less than the number the call gives.

      ~(5 | lt 10 | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/lt/than
  |~~ The value the subject is compared with. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:number/lt/than/NotNumberError |~~ The than is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/lt/less |~~ Whether the subject comes before the value compared. ~~|
  ::boolean

:number/lte
  |~~ Whether the subject is less than or equal to the number the call
      gives.

      ~(10 | lte 10 | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:number/lte/than
  |~~ The value the subject is compared with. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:number/lte/than/NotNumberError |~~ The than is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:number/lte/atMost |~~ Whether the subject comes before the value compared or equals it. ~~|
  ::boolean
